// Practice quiz: The N+1 query problem: spotting it in SQL logs
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/relationships-and-transactions/the-n-1-query-problem-spotting-it-in-sql-logs

::o1 pattern::What does the N stand for in N+1? {
=The number of parent rows, each triggering a child query#Correct. One extra query per parent.
~The number of tables#Not quite. Tables are not the count.
~The number of columns#Not quite. Columns are not the count.
~The number of threads#Not quite. Threads are unrelated.
}

::o1 reading the log::In a SQL log, what is the signature of N+1? {
=The same select repeated with a different bound value#Correct. Each repetition serves one parent.
~One long join#Not quite. A join avoids the pattern.
~A single update#Not quite. Updates are a different matter.
~A startup banner#Not quite. That is unrelated.
}

::o2 counting::For a listing of 200 parents, how many queries does an N+1 pattern issue? {
=201#Correct. One for the parents, and 200 for the children.
~200#Not quite. You also need the first query.
~2#Not quite. That would be a batched or fetched approach.
~1#Not quite. That would be a single fetch join.
}

::o2 statistics::What gives a counted number of JDBC statements per session? {
=Hibernate generate_statistics#Correct. It reports statements for the session.
~server.port#Not quite. That sets a port.
~logging of the Spring banner#Not quite. That shows nothing about SQL.
~A larger page size#Not quite. That changes rows, not reporting.
}

::o3 hidden by data::Why do small test datasets often hide N+1? {
=A few extra queries run so fast that nobody notices#Correct. The cost scales with row count.
~Tests turn off lazy loading#Not quite. They do not.
~H2 cannot run joins#Not quite. H2 can run joins.
~Tests skip the SQL layer#Not quite. They still run SQL.
}

